WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.757 --> 00:00:06.840
GREG CASTLE: Hi, I'm Greg.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.970
I'm the Anthos GKE security
tech lead at Google.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.660
SAMRAT RAY: Hi, I'm Samrat Ray.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.210
I'm the Anthos security product
management lead at Google.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.630
GREG CASTLE: So today we're
talking about Anthos security.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.340
We wanted to start
with the takeaways

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.840
that we're going to get
from this presentation.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.840
First up is we want you
to be able to understand

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.200
how Anthos improves security
across a hybrid production

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.860
environment.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.740
Next is there's a lot
of products and features

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:36.260
in this presentation--
there's about 30 of them.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.110
So we want to make
sure that we're

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.870
being really obvious and
deliberate about where

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.330
each feature is available and
which environment on which

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.590
platform it's available.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.970
Really, the high-level thing
we want to leave you with here

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.310
is a four-step
high-level workflow

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.770
to configure Anthos
security across

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.440
multiple different platforms.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.000
Just a quick note on names--

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.250
we've shortened some of the
names in this presentation

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.080
just to make things
a bit easier to say

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.620
and to fit them on diagrams,
so using GKE for Anthos

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:07.550
on Google cloud.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.150
This presentation is called
"Modernize Your Security

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:13.650
Posture."

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.870
So what's being modernized?

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.620
And what industry
trends are driving that?

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.140
We think these three
things are happening.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.350
First is there's not just
one production security

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.030
infrastructure to
harden anymore.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:28.960
There's multiple-- on-prem
and there's multiple clouds.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.810
Second-- Kubernetes
and containers

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.910
have really changed a lot
of our security assumptions

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:35.980
about production infrastructure.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.910
So previously we might
have had an application

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:43.180
running on a virtual machine
with always the same IP,

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.010
whereas in Kubernetes
and containers,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:48.280
we have applications
being dynamically scaled,

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.010
scheduled across different
virtual machines,

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.030
and with dynamically
allocated IPs.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.390
And that's quite a difference
in security properties.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.960
We also have these big monoliths
that have been broken down

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.640
into microservices.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.500
And all of those
microservices now

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.570
need authentication
and authorization

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.020
to be able to talk
to each other.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:09.070
And that needs to happen
inside the perimeter.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:13.320
So this is where Anthos really
starts to come in and help.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.320
If we look at this hybrid
infrastructure where we have

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.960
clusters running on Google
Cloud, other clouds,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.187
and on-prem, the four points at
the top are really where Anthos

00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:24.380
is helping you--

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.190
at the infrastructure level,
at a separate policy layer,

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.330
with services and
application security,

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.100
and with monitoring
and detection.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.090
And so we took those
four kind of concepts

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.910
and we turned them
into these four steps

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.400
that you can use to
harden applications

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:46.050
and get secure deployments
across a hybrid infrastructure.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:50.480
So the first step we're going
to talk through is hardening--

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.190
how do you do
patching, compliance,

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.240
hardening configuration.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.340
Next, guardrails-- how do you
segment, isolate, and apply

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.190
that policy across
lots of clusters?

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:04.040
Securing workloads-- how do
you only run what you trust

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.980
and have really
fine-grained authorization?

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.450
And monitoring detection--
once you've done all of that,

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.750
how do you find
misconfigurations and detect

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.222
attackers doing bad
things to clusters?

00:03:14.222 --> 00:03:16.430
We want to be able to do
these four things across all

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.610
of your different environments.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:21.690
So let's start with
hardening the infrastructure.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.148
And the spoiler
for this section is

00:03:23.148 --> 00:03:25.080
we've basically done
most of this for you,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.890
but we're going to talk through
a whole lot of work that's

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.515
happening behind the
scenes so that you

00:03:28.515 --> 00:03:30.180
know that it's happening.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.330
So starting with security
patching and hardening,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:38.130
if we look at a big stack
of technology for Kubernetes

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.350
clusters, everything from
the hardware right up

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.390
to application
containers, each one

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.700
of these layers at
some point probably

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.700
needs security patching, and
it definitely needs hardening.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.560
So when we're running on our
own infrastructure with GKE,

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.170
we've basically taking the
responsibility for most

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.020
of that technology stack.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.112
For our Anthos that isn't
GKE, so on-prem and AWS,

00:04:02.112 --> 00:04:04.070
we're selling a little
further up because we're

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.340
running on that infrastructure
that the customers provided,

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:10.730
so the VMs running on-prem
or the virtual machines

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.370
running in AWS or other clouds.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.290
So it's more of a
responsibility sandwich,

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:17.360
we like to call this one.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.029
But we're still doing a lot
of work in the middle there.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.150
So where we do control
that infrastructure on GKE,

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:28.300
we can deliver some pretty cool
advanced security functionality

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:32.130
that's on by default or is
exposed with really simplified

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.780
configuration.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.480
So some of the things we're
doing, moving from the bottom

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.660
up in this diagram, the
first thing we'll talk about

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.530
is Shielded VMs and nodes.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:44.730
So these features give
us verifiable integrity

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.340
from our Titan
hardware security chip

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.410
and the integrity of
the underlying firmware

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:53.880
and up through the kernel.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.640
And then we have
the virtual TPM that

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.560
gives us a secure
bootstrap and a way

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.460
to join the node to
the cluster securely.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.860
Customer managed encryption
keys for disk encryption.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.360
So every VM is
encrypted automatically

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.570
by default in Google
Cloud with a key that is

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.520
managed by Google internally.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:19.640
If you want to re-encryt that
VM again, re-encrypt the disk

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.540
again, you can do that using
managed encryption keys

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.360
that you store in Cloud KMS.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.310
And a similar feature
for Kubernetes secrets.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:32.340
You can encrypt Kubernetes
secrets using a Cloud

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.620
KMS managed key as well.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.500
The control plane and the node--
the cluster infrastructure

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.190
itself--

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:43.107
automatically upgrades
itself by default on GKE.

00:05:43.107 --> 00:05:45.190
So if you create a cluster
and just leave it there

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.740
and do nothing to it, it'll
just keep upgrading itself

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:53.470
and keep itself in a good,
security configuration.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:56.170
We also offer some control
over how those upgrades happen.

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:58.600
We have different
release channels--

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.250
rapid, regular, and
stable-- that you

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.640
can choose to
really sort of dial

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.070
up or down how quickly
those updates are

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:07.490
happening on your clusters.

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:10.030
There's also additional features
like maintenance windows

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:10.900
and exclusions.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.800
So let's talk a little
bit about compliance

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.240
starting with the
Kubernetes CIS benchmark.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.000
So the Center for
Internet Security

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.190
has created a
Kubernetes benchmark

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:25.760
that tells you how to run
Kubernetes in a hardened

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:26.960
configuration.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:29.720
There's really a lot of
settings in this benchmark.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:34.670
And we've done the hard work
to configure all those settings

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:38.720
in the best possible
way, and we've

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:44.290
published our full results
for GKE and for on-prem.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:47.110
We've also developed a
GKE-specific CIS benchmark

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.150
that basically just
takes our hardening guide

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.840
and gives you that
in a checklist form.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:56.108
The infrastructure
itself, we've had

00:06:56.108 --> 00:06:57.650
a bunch of compliance
coverage there,

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.740
so various standards
listed here.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:01.820
This is for GKE.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:05.080
We're also working on compliance
coverage for infrastructure

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.300
in the other hybrid environments
that we're offering.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.090
And we expect to make some
more announcements there soon.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:12.930
That's the infrastructure layer.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.870
The next layer up when you want
to build an application that

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.700
is itself compliant, we're then
providing some solution guides

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.790
such as for PCI for
GKE that basically

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.520
help you write that
PCI application on top

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.838
of GKE infrastructure.

00:07:28.838 --> 00:07:30.630
And we're also working
on additional guides

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.630
for additional platforms
and standards here too.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.963
So summing up this
section, Anthos really

00:07:37.963 --> 00:07:39.380
does most of the
work for you here

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:41.250
across all of the platforms.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:45.088
If you follow our guidelines
for multitenancy on GKE,

00:07:45.088 --> 00:07:46.880
there's a bit of extra
information in there

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.060
about how to structure projects,
how to put them in folders,

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.500
and how to create DPCs.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.450
For on-prem and
GKE, you can then

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.753
follow the hardening guides to
create the clusters themselves.

00:07:57.753 --> 00:07:59.670
And on top of that,
follow the solution guides

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.900
to make the applications
that run in clusters.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:05.615
All the links are provided here.

00:08:05.615 --> 00:08:06.990
So once you've
made some clusters

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.910
and have some
infrastructure to use,

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.920
you really want to
establish some guardrails

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.795
around that infrastructure
and make sure it's

00:08:12.795 --> 00:08:15.460
being used appropriately.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.340
The really key feature here
is Anthos Config Management.

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:20.080
And this gives us
the ability to manage

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:24.260
cross-cluster and cross-cloud
a lot of different policies.

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:27.910
And the idea here is
to use a GitOps model

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:31.870
where our infrastructure
as code is really checked

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:33.350
into our source control.

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.980
So the idea there
is that when we're

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.000
using source control to
manage infrastructure,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.850
we have automatic review.

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:40.820
We have a history.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:42.429
We have a way to
templatize and create

00:08:42.429 --> 00:08:45.700
new pieces of
infrastructure easily.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:49.660
So looking at how
this works for Anthos,

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:51.970
we used Terraform
to make the kind

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.760
of base layer of infrastructure,
things like projects

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.630
and VPCs and clusters.

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:59.110
And then the next layer
is where ACM comes in.

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:02.920
So ACM Config Sync is generally
available across all three

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:04.750
Anthos platforms here.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:08.540
And that gives you the ability
to create namespaces, configure

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:12.190
RBAC, security
controls, and also

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.680
create identities
through service accounts,

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.920
and really actually manage
any Kubernetes object at scale

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:23.200
across many clusters across all
the hybrid environments here.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.160
You can also go even further.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:28.690
And if you like the declarative
kind of [? configures ?]

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:33.100
code approach, you can use it to
also create your GCP resources

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:34.800
using GCP config connector.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.660
And the idea here
is that if you want

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.880
to-- if your application
needs a Pub/Sub instance,

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.360
you could write a
piece of YAML that

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:46.540
goes along with all of your
other YAMLs for Kubernetes.

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:51.280
And you can try a Kube control
apply, all of those at once

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.715
and have both the pod started
as well as the GCP resources

00:09:54.715 --> 00:09:56.690
that those applications need.

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:59.140
That's also GA across
GKE and on-prem.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.760
So ACM config sync
kind of sets us up

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:09.110
in this base infrastructure
security configuration.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.510
Policy controller
is a feature that

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.420
also works across all
three environments here,

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.640
and it allows us
to really control

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:20.950
what the pods are doing
inside that cluster.

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:23.635
So the idea here is
you want to make sure

00:10:23.635 --> 00:10:26.010
that containers aren't doing
things like running as root,

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:30.990
unaccessing the host network,
unaccessing the host powers,

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:32.880
and doing other
dangerous things.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.640
So we give you a really
comprehensive policy library

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.400
that you can apply
to your clusters.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.480
And that policy
library as an example

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:41.520
here of not running as root--

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.810
that policy library
is written using

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:49.030
open policy agent's gatekeeper,
so a open source policy

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:49.530
language.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:56.660
So both of those features
are available across all

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.150
the environments to use today.

00:10:58.150 --> 00:10:59.780
They're all GA.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:02.660
There's a couple of extra things
we can talk about on GKE when

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:05.050
you're running on GKE itself.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:06.620
The first is org policies.

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:09.430
So org policies sit above
all of your clusters,

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:10.930
above all your projects.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.630
And they allow you to
have like a macro policy

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:15.980
control across many objects.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:19.138
So one thing you can do is
restrict regions for resources.

00:11:19.138 --> 00:11:20.680
So if you just want
to run in the US,

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:24.430
you can make an org policy that
says that for Google Cloud.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:27.280
Another thing you can do
for your Kubernetes clusters

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:31.240
is to restrict how services
are exposed to the internet.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.760
Most organizations want to
avoid someone accidentally

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:38.830
putting a untested or a kind of
poorly configured application

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:40.300
accidentally on the internet.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:42.092
And this is a way to
make sure that doesn't

00:11:42.092 --> 00:11:44.035
happen with your GKE clusters.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:51.000
Another guardrail of
technology on GKE cloud

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.370
is VPC service controls.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:57.180
This technology allows us to
have some confidence that when

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:58.740
we've put some
information in cloud

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:02.520
storage or other
similar cloud resources

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.800
on GCP that it's
not going to easily

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:07.580
be exfiltrated by a bad guy.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:10.110
So there's a few scenarios
that it protects against.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:13.890
Imagine someone accidentally
messing up a access control

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:15.990
rule on a cloud storage
bucket and making

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:17.990
it public for everyone to read.

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:20.265
The VPC would still
protect that scenario,

00:12:20.265 --> 00:12:22.140
because there's a
perimeter around there that

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.410
says those cloud storage
buckets should only be accessed

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:27.970
from authorized VPCs.

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:30.910
In a similar fashion, if one
of your computer resources

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:37.540
happen to get
compromised, it would also

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:39.700
make it more difficult to
exfiltrate that data out

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:43.770
from the cloud by establishing
the same perimeter

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.210
and only allowing the
traffic to flow out

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.700
on the authorized VPCs.

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:52.720
So private GKE clusters
can be established

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:54.200
inside the perimeter.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.120
So they're not on the internet.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:00.250
You can also do the same
with Container Registry

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:02.500
with your container images.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.140
And VPC service
controls supports

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:10.550
hybrid in that you can connect
your hybrid on-prem networks

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:16.010
by using Cloud
Interconnect or cloud VPN.

00:13:16.010 --> 00:13:18.650
So just summing up
this section, we

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:21.980
want to use ACM to do the
vast majority of our policy

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:25.340
configuration enforcement across
our hybrid infrastructure.

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:27.680
Then on GKE cloud, we
have some extra controls

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:32.670
that we can use to establish
some extra guardrails.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:36.530
So that's the end of
the guardrails section.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:38.180
I'm now going to hand
it over to Samrat

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:41.620
to talk about how to
secure our workloads.

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:42.880
SAMRAT RAY: Thanks, Greg.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:44.880
We have now covered
how to configure

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.770
a secure infrastructure
and set up guardrails.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:49.660
You are now ready
to deploy workloads

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:51.910
to this secure landing zone.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:56.320
Let us look at how you can
secure workloads with Anthos.

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:59.170
Anthos provides tools
to implement a defense

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:02.260
in depth architecture, or
a zero trust architecture,

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:04.990
if you like.

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:07.090
We have provided here
four steps that you

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:10.420
can use to secure
workloads on Anthos,

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:14.410
the first being
securing your build

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.720
and deployment
pipelines, ensuring

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:20.620
that your entire supply chain
for your code from source

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:22.810
to your production is secure.

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:25.970
The second is to ensure
that all workloads deployed

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:30.610
to production are appropriately
isolated from the internet

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:34.660
and from each other within
your production [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:39.310
You should also isolate
your pods on the node

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:44.300
if multiple applications are
deployed on a single cluster.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:47.800
Lastly, you want to
ensure that services

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:49.600
that are exposed
to the internet are

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:52.630
done so with appropriate
protection against internet

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:53.300
facing attacks.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:58.250
So let's go through
the first step, which

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:00.740
is to secure workloads.

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:02.480
The objective of
securing workloads

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.210
is to ensure that
only trusted code can

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.310
be deployed to production.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:12.890
To ensure that, we recommend you
lock down your CICD pipelines,

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:16.430
ensure that you use
a secure base image,

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:21.200
scan that base build
image for vulnerabilities.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:23.330
Google Cloud's
Container Registry

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:26.300
provides incremental and
continuous monitoring

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.540
against a known
vulnerability database.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:33.710
The output of the scans
are available as metadata

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:36.060
with your pod.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:39.180
The fact that you have gone
through all the above steps

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:42.540
can be asserted using
binary authorization.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:45.780
Binary authorization allows
you to define a policy

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:50.265
that checks for attestation
from each step of your

00:15:50.265 --> 00:15:52.020
build pipeline.

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:54.390
You can leverage
the tools provided

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:58.710
by GCP for building and
deploying your containers.

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:03.690
Or you can bring your own
build and deployment tools

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:08.070
as long as they have
testers that are trusted

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:10.520
by binary authorization.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.220
Only workloads that have
the right attestations

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:15.440
can be deployed to production.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:18.350
All deployment and
denials are audited

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:20.800
by binary authorization.

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:23.890
You can now trust the
code running in production

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:27.910
with a privileged identity.

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:31.870
The next step is to
isolate the deployed code

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:33.130
from the internet.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:36.870
This can be achieved by ensuring
that all clusters are private

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:39.640
and any service that is
exposed to the internet

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:44.290
is done so using an internet
facing load balancer.

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:47.530
Google Cloud's load balancer
provides industry-leading DDoS

00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:48.820
protection.

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:53.080
Further, you can configure web
application firewall policies

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:55.240
via Cloud Armor.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:58.600
You can also configure
zero trust access

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:03.430
by your employees using
identity via proxy.

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:06.880
In addition to isolating the
applications from the internet,

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:11.410
it is also necessary to isolate
workloads from each other

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:14.520
to prevent the lateral
movement of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:18.400
We'd need to prevent lateral
movements over the network,

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.480
but also between pods
on the same node.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.430
Anthos provides
two technologies--

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:26.550
Anthos service mesh and
Kubernetes network policy

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:29.220
to isolate workloads
on the network.

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:31.680
Anthos also provides
two technologies--

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:34.110
GKE Sandbox and AppArmor--

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.330
to isolate applications
on the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:40.400
Let's learn more about
these technologies.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:42.980
A common approach with
Anthos is to create

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:45.810
clusters that are shared
across multiple teams.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.990
Each team gets one
or more name spaces

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:50.870
into which to deploy their
application workloads.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:54.570
Thus, namespaces form a
natural boundary for isolation.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:57.800
Kubernetes network policy allows
you to isolate applications

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:01.700
on a single cluster
based on namespaces.

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:04.670
You can then define
ingress and egress rules

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:08.990
based on IP CIDRs or Kubernetes
objects such as namespaces

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:11.490
and labels.

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:16.560
This approach can be called
as micro-segmentation.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:19.020
It's a good approach if you
don't have too many exceptions

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:21.250
to manage.

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:24.430
However, in many cases, we
have seen through experience

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:27.310
that there are a lot of
east-west communications

00:18:27.310 --> 00:18:29.140
between applications.

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:33.540
And we implement shared
services which break isolation.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.240
Thus, we recommend using
coarse-grained segments

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:39.568
for mandatory controls and
defining fine-grained policies

00:18:39.568 --> 00:18:40.610
at the application level.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:46.230
So you can define
application-level policies

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:49.800
and an end-to-end zero
trust access solution

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:53.460
using Anthos service mesh
and identity via proxy.

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:56.580
With Anthos service
mesh, all communications

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.380
are encrypted in transit.

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:01.450
Further, all
workload-to-workload

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:04.090
communications can be
strongly authenticated,

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:06.560
authorized, and logged.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:09.610
Workloads are authenticated
using certificates

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:12.040
instead of bare tokens.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:16.090
Using mutual TLS for
authentication helps mitigate

00:19:16.090 --> 00:19:19.660
the risk of impersonation and
privilege escalation through

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.600
replay or
man-in-the-middle attacks.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:27.550
ASM also provides you with
context of our policies

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:32.890
that allows you to define access
controls based on peer request

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:34.760
and application contexts.

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:38.230
For example, you can state that
only credit card front end can

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:40.390
access credit card back end.

00:19:40.390 --> 00:19:42.610
You do this in
terms of identities.

00:19:42.610 --> 00:19:45.820
Thus, credit card front end
can be deployed anywhere,

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:48.790
and access policy remains
the same irrespective

00:19:48.790 --> 00:19:51.670
of the network context
of credit card front end.

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:54.250
You can also state that
credit card front end

00:19:54.250 --> 00:19:58.390
can only access PII
when it presents

00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:00.610
an end user credential, i.e.

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:04.510
a valid JWT token that
has the right claims.

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:06.670
Thus, with Anthos
service mesh, you

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:09.310
can reduce the privilege of
the credit card front end

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:12.340
with respect to
PII, but also ensure

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:15.430
that all access to
your credit card data

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:18.780
happens only through the
credit card front end.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:22.140
On GCP, we provide
identity via proxy

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:23.760
for authenticating
and authorizing

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:27.010
user access based on
BeyondCorp principles.

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:31.500
You can also use your own
user authentication solution

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:34.340
with Anthos service mesh.

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:37.083
And you can do all of
this without changing

00:20:37.083 --> 00:20:38.000
your application code.

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:42.790
Depending on your
security profile

00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:44.950
and what your
application is, you

00:20:44.950 --> 00:20:46.810
might want a second
layer of isolation

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:50.740
between your container
workloads on a shared host.

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:54.450
Anthos provides two
solutions for isolating

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:57.210
pods on a shared node.

00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:00.710
The first one is GKE Sandbox,
which is available on GCP.

00:21:00.710 --> 00:21:04.410
And the second is AppArmor,
which is now in beta,

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:07.200
but is available on
all environments.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:10.350
GKE Sandbox is based upon
an open source project

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:14.520
gVisor that uses a user space
kernel to intercept and handle

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:15.690
[? cis ?] calls.

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:18.240
Thus, you're reducing the
interaction with the host

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:21.220
and reducing the attack surface.

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:25.060
We have now made this
available to you in Anthos

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:31.510
as the GKE Sandbox, which can be
enabled with a one-line change.

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:33.920
With AppArmor, on
the other hand,

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:36.980
you can define and
reduce the privileges

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:39.110
accessible by an application.

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:42.630
You need to define
application-specific profiles

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:43.270
with AppArmor.

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:50.330
So let us now look at a
summary of this section.

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:53.150
So we provide a defense
in depth approach

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:56.690
to secure your
workloads from securing

00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:59.330
your build and
deployment pipelines

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:02.160
to isolating your
applications on the network.

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:04.220
And we can do that with
a different in depth

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:07.520
approach using
segmentation and zero trust

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:09.470
approach with
Anthos service mesh

00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:12.800
and isolating your
pods on a shared node.

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:15.500
And finally, we
provide you the ability

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:19.660
to protect your internet exposed
services using Google Cloud

00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:23.260
load balancer and CloudArmor.

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:25.360
Now let us look
at the fourth step

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:28.870
to secure your
applications with Anthos.

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:31.780
The fourth step is to enable
monitoring and detection

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:35.360
for any incidents.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:38.710
Now, you have done your best to
configure everything securely,

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:41.510
but how do you know
you did it right?

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.640
In order to know
that, we provide you

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:44.870
with the three-layered stack.

00:22:44.870 --> 00:22:47.930
Going from the
bottom, we provide you

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:52.040
with Security Health Analytics
and the Security Scanner that

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:55.370
analyzes your configuration
to prevent threats

00:22:55.370 --> 00:22:57.440
because of a misconfiguration.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:01.800
The layer above that is a set of
threat detection capabilities,

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:04.310
which is the container threat
detection, event threat

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:07.060
detection, and
Access Transparency.

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:09.700
Access Transparency
enables you to understand

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:15.250
how a Google administrator may
have access to your resources.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:19.540
And all of this information is
available through a single pane

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:25.100
of glass via the Cloud
Security Command Center.

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:27.800
If you want to bring your
own visibility tools,

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:29.390
we allow you to do that.

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:33.620
And you can integrate
those tools at two levels.

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:36.200
You can export all
your allowed events

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:40.520
and logs that you need via
Pub/Sub for easy integration,

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:45.080
and use your own security
visibility and analysis tools

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:46.770
with Anthos.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:49.490
So let's understand a bit
more about Security Health

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:51.090
Analytics.

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:53.660
Security Health Analytics
helps prevent threats

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:55.820
by telling you about
problems before they

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:57.580
get exploited by an attacker.

00:23:57.580 --> 00:24:01.820
SHA alerts you to
misconfiguration errors.

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:04.970
In the context of
GKE, an example

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:07.490
is an old cluster running
an insecure authentication

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:09.480
mechanism.

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:11.930
We also provide Web
Security Scanner,

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:14.700
which identifies issues
with web applications.

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:18.560
The results of the analysis
are displayed in Cloud Security

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:19.520
Command Center.

00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:23.830
Another tool to understand is
the Container Threat Detection.

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:27.310
Container Threat Detection helps
you detect potential attacks

00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:32.170
in real time by monitoring
for suspicious behavior

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:34.300
inside containers
that will indicate

00:24:34.300 --> 00:24:35.680
that they are compromised.

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:38.590
These include things like
downloading and running

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:40.080
new binaries.

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:41.830
In addition to container
threat detection,

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:45.010
we also provide event threat
detection for suspicious events

00:24:45.010 --> 00:24:49.860
and logs, such as traffic
to known bad domains and IPs

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:51.970
and crypto-mining.

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:54.740
Again, you can see the findings
in Cloud Security Command

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:56.070
Center.

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:59.400
To summarize this
section on GCP,

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:03.000
we provide a number of tools to
help you analyze your posture

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:05.160
and detect incidents.

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:09.300
This information is available
in Cloud Security Command Center

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:11.803
or a tool of your choice.

00:25:11.803 --> 00:25:14.220
You can view all your alerts
in the Cloud Security Command

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:15.890
Center.

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:19.190
And to recap what we
have just covered,

00:25:19.190 --> 00:25:22.660
there are four steps to securing
your modern applications

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:23.890
using Anthos.

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:26.330
You harden your infrastructure.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:29.470
You then establish guardrails.

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:31.660
Once you have a
secured landing zone,

00:25:31.660 --> 00:25:34.450
you can now secure
your workloads

00:25:34.450 --> 00:25:37.930
by securing your
deployment pipelines

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:42.580
and isolating your workloads
in your production environment.

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:45.790
And finally, you enable
monitoring and detection

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:48.010
to ensure that you
have the right posture

00:25:48.010 --> 00:25:51.520
and that you are
alerted on incidents.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:54.520
And you can enable
this four-step workflow

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:57.640
across all your
environments, whether it's

00:25:57.640 --> 00:26:05.130
GCP or on-premise on AWS
and others in the future.

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:06.850
Thank you.

